{"task_id": "Mbpp/84", "solution": "def sequence(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the nth number in the Newman-Conway sequence using dynamic programming.\n    The sequence is defined by P(1)=1, P(2)=1, and P(n) = P(P(n-1)) + P(n-P(n-1)) for n > 2.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n <= 0:\n        # Assuming valid positive integer input based on design context,\n        # but including a safety return for non-positive for completeness\n        # without raising an error, as per explicit constraint.\n        return 0 \n    if n == 1 or n == 2:\n        return 1\n\n    # Use a list for memoization, as indices are contiguous.\n    # dp[0] is unused, dp[i] stores P(i).\n    dp = [0] * (n + 1)\n    dp[1] = 1\n    dp[2] = 1\n\n    for i in range(3, n + 1):\n        # P(n) = P(P(n-1)) + P(n-P(n-1))\n        # translated to dp: dp[i] = dp[dp[i-1]] + dp[i - dp[i-1]]\n        \n        # We need to ensure that the indices for dp lookup are within bounds.\n        # Since dp values for i-1 are always positive (1 or more) and less than i,\n        # dp[i-1] will be a valid index previously calculated.\n        # i - dp[i-1] must also be a valid index (>=1).\n        # The Newman-Conway sequence has P(n) >= 1 for n >= 1.\n        # And P(n-P(n-1)) implies n - P(n-1) must be >= 1.\n        # This property holds for the Newman-Conway sequence itself.\n        \n        val_at_prev_idx = dp[i-1]\n        \n        first_term_idx = val_at_prev_idx\n        second_term_idx = i - val_at_prev_idx\n        \n        dp[i] = dp[first_term_idx] + dp[second_term_idx]\n\n    return dp[n]"}
