{"task_id": "Mbpp/237", "solution": "import collections\nfrom typing import List, Tuple, Dict\n\ndef normalize_tuple(t: Tuple) -> Tuple:\n    \"\"\"Sorts the elements within a tuple to create a normalized representation.\"\"\"\n    return tuple(sorted(t))\n\ndef check_occurences(tuple_list: List[Tuple]) -> Dict[Tuple, int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Counts the occurrences of unique tuples in a list, treating tuples with the same elements in different orders as equivalent.\n    Tuples are normalized by sorting their elements before counting.\n    \"\"\"\n    normalized_tuples = [normalize_tuple(t) for t in tuple_list]\n    occurrence_counts = collections.Counter(normalized_tuples)\n    return dict(occurrence_counts)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/239", "solution": "def get_total_number_of_sequences(m: int, n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the number of possible sequences of length n, such that each element is a\n    positive integer, greater than or equal to twice the previous element, and less than or equal to m.\n    \"\"\"\n    # dp[val] will store the number of sequences of the current length ending with 'val'.\n    # We use a 1-indexed array for values from 1 to m.\n    prev_counts = [0] * (m + 1)\n\n    # Base case: For sequences of length 1, any positive integer from 1 to m is valid.\n    for val in range(1, m + 1):\n        prev_counts[val] = 1\n\n    # Iterate for sequence length from 2 up to n.\n    for _ in range(2, n + 1):\n        current_counts = [0] * (m + 1)\n        \n        # Calculate prefix sums of prev_counts to efficiently sum previous elements.\n        # prefix_sum[p] = sum(prev_counts[1]...prev_counts[p])\n        prefix_sum = [0] * (m + 1)\n        for val in range(1, m + 1):\n            prefix_sum[val] = prefix_sum[val - 1] + prev_counts[val]\n\n        # Fill current_counts for the new sequence length.\n        # For each current_val, it can be preceded by any prev_val such that current_val >= 2 * prev_val.\n        # This means prev_val <= current_val / 2.\n        for current_val in range(1, m + 1):\n            max_prev_val = current_val // 2\n            # Sum counts for all valid previous values (from 1 up to max_prev_val)\n            if max_prev_val >= 1:\n                current_counts[current_val] = prefix_sum[max_prev_val]\n        \n        prev_counts = current_counts\n\n    # The total number of sequences of length n is the sum of all counts for the last length.\n    return sum(prev_counts)"}
