{"task_id": "Mbpp/306", "solution": "def max_sum_increasing_subseq(array: list[int], length: int, i: int, k: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the maximum sum of an increasing subsequence from prefix until ith index\n    and including the element at index k (where k > i).\n    \"\"\"\n    # dp_prefix[x] stores the maximum sum of an increasing subsequence\n    # ending at array[x], considering only elements up to index i.\n    # The size is i + 1, covering indices 0 to i.\n    dp_prefix = [0] * (i + 1)\n\n    # Calculate max sum increasing subsequence for the prefix up to index i\n    for x in range(i + 1):\n        # The subsequence can always start with array[x] itself\n        dp_prefix[x] = array[x]\n        # Check previous elements to extend existing increasing subsequences\n        for j in range(x):\n            if array[x] > array[j]:\n                dp_prefix[x] = max(dp_prefix[x], dp_prefix[j] + array[x])\n\n    # Now, find the maximum sum from dp_prefix that can precede array[k]\n    # 'best_prefix_sum' will store the maximum sum of an increasing subsequence\n    # ending at some array[j] (where j <= i) such that array[j] < array[k].\n    # Initialize to 0, representing an empty prefix if no suitable preceding element is found.\n    best_prefix_sum = 0\n\n    # Iterate through the calculated prefix sums\n    for j in range(i + 1):\n        # If array[j] can be an element immediately preceding array[k] in an increasing subsequence\n        if array[j] < array[k]:\n            best_prefix_sum = max(best_prefix_sum, dp_prefix[j])\n\n    # The final maximum sum is the best prefix sum found plus array[k].\n    # If no suitable prefix was found (best_prefix_sum remains 0), the sum would be array[k] itself.\n    return best_prefix_sum + array[k]"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/308", "solution": "def large_product(list1, list2, n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the specified number of largest products from two lists, selecting one factor from each.\n    Calculates all possible products, sorts them in descending order, and returns the top N as integers.\n    \"\"\"\n    all_products = []\n    for x in list1:\n        for y in list2:\n            all_products.append(x * y)\n\n    all_products.sort(reverse=True)\n\n    # Select the top 'n' products and ensure they are integers.\n    result = [int(p) for p in all_products[:n]]\n\n    return result"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/311", "solution": "def _find_leftmost_unset_bit(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the position of the leftmost unset bit (highest index) within the significant bits of n.\n    If n is 0, returns 0. If all significant bits up to n.bit_length()-1 are set, returns n.bit_length().\n    \"\"\"\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    # Iterate from the highest set bit's position (n.bit_length() - 1) down to 0.\n    # n.bit_length() gives the number of bits required to represent n, so the highest index is n.bit_length() - 1.\n    for pos in range(n.bit_length() - 1, -1, -1):\n        if (n >> pos) & 1 == 0:  # If the bit at 'pos' is unset (0)\n            return pos\n    \n    # If the loop completes, it means all bits from n.bit_length() - 1 down to 0 are set.\n    # In this case, the leftmost unset bit is the next available position, which is n.bit_length().\n    return n.bit_length()\n\ndef _set_bit_at_position(n: int, pos: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Sets the bit at the given position 'pos' in 'n' to 1.\n    This is achieved using a bitwise OR operation with a mask (1 shifted left by 'pos').\n    \"\"\"\n    return n | (1 << pos)\n\ndef set_left_most_unset_bit(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Sets the leftmost unset bit in the binary representation of the input integer n.\n    The leftmost unset bit is defined as the highest-indexed bit `pos` such that\n    the `pos`-th bit is 0, considering bits up to and including `n.bit_length()`.\n    \"\"\"\n    bit_pos = _find_left_most_unset_bit(n)\n    return _set_bit_at_position(n, bit_pos)"}
