{"task_id": "Mbpp/605", "solution": "import math\n\ndef prime_num(n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if an integer is a prime number.\n    Returns True if n is prime, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n <= 1:\n        return False\n    if n == 2:\n        return True\n    if n % 2 == 0:\n        return False\n    # Check for divisibility by odd numbers from 3 up to sqrt(n)\n    for i in range(3, int(math.sqrt(n)) + 1, 2):\n        if n % i == 0:\n            return False\n    return True"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/603", "solution": "def get_ludic(limit: int) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Generates all lucid numbers up to a given limit using a sieve-based algorithm.\n    Returns an empty list for inputs less than 1.\n    \"\"\"\n    if limit < 1:\n        return []\n\n    numbers = list(range(1, limit + 1))\n    lucid_numbers = []\n    \n    while numbers:\n        current_lucid = numbers[0]\n        lucid_numbers.append(current_lucid)\n        \n        if len(numbers) == 1:\n            break\n\n        # Sieve step: Remove every current_lucid-th number from the remaining sequence\n        # The indices to remove are 0-based, so for current_lucid=1, remove 0th (which is 1 itself, already added)\n        # For current_lucid=2, remove 1st, 3rd, 5th... (2nd, 4th, 6th number in the sequence)\n        \n        # We need to build a new list, as removing elements while iterating by index is problematic\n        next_numbers = []\n        removal_indices = set()\n        for i in range(current_lucid - 1, len(numbers), current_lucid):\n            removal_indices.add(i)\n        \n        for i, num in enumerate(numbers):\n            if i not in removal_indices:\n                next_numbers.append(num)\n        \n        numbers = next_numbers\n        \n    return lucid_numbers"}
